commit 79ed8ee3def7ad659ae125aa9cb2e7006d638c3a
Author: kunchenguid <[redacted email]>
Date:   Tue Sep 15 10:36:44 2026 -0700

    feat(now): the task record opens as a pane beside the panel, tied to its row

    The captain's call on the detail-pane prototype (FI3-102, card action
    "Build it in the app"): clicking a now-panel row used to make the task
    record the FRONT ENTRY OF THE CENTRE CARD STACK, so the detail landed in
    the middle of the window, over the character, in the same pager as
    unrelated cards - far from the row it came from. "it pops up a card. i
    find that a bit frictional."

    It is now a pane docked in the right rail's gutter:

    - top-anchored to the row it was opened from and clamped into the window,
      tied to the row's CENTRE by a hairline and a notch that track it as the
      list scrolls while the pane holds still; a row scrolled out of the
      panel's viewport drops the tie rather than dragging the pane;
    - the row reads as selected - an accent-tinted surface, the title at full
      strength, and no border at all (the captain's revision);
    - up to 520px and growing with the window (also the captain's revision),
      because the record's prose read badly in a rail-width column;
    - out of the card stack entirely: it no longer pages with the cards, no
      longer claims the stage (`derivePhase` ignores it, so the character is
      not dimmed), and a decision card can now sit on screen beside it. A turn
      carries its task id ALONGSIDE the shown card's id, because both are up.

    The row's highlight is painted inside the panel's sandboxed frame while the
    shell owns what is open, and nothing could push host state into a frame, so
    bridge v2 gains one additive host-to-frame verb next to the theme:
    `fm.state` / `fm.onState`, re-pushed on every document birth. It is not a
    read - a read is core-served app data with declared invalidation, and
    "which row is open" is none of those. The bridge script's text changed, so
    FRAME_BRIDGE_SHA256 moved with it and every frame CSP carries the new hash
    (frameBridgeHash.test.ts pins the pair); this is the bridge surface
    docs/live-prototype-security.md reviews, so that review wants a re-read.

    A panel action also now learns the frame's own rect, the one thing only the
    host knows, which is how the pane is placed against a row it cannot see.
